From 00ba7049bcd1067a47afe1919745f174c93dec21 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andre Watson <dre@ligandal.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 21:50:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 10/32] fix(qsa): one max-width MTP selection buffer serves
 every adaptive state

index_share_for_mtp_iteration was switched off entirely under adaptive speculation,
on the grounds that a state swap between the draft-extend capture and the decode
lookup would leave per-state index buffers mismatched. The premise is right; the
conclusion is stronger than it needs to be. Two changes make the feature adaptive-safe:

1. ONE shared QSAMTPSharedSparseIndices, cached on the draft worker and reused by
   every state's backends, so capture and lookup address the same buffer across a
   swap. init_attention_backend runs once per state, which is why the buffer has
   to be cached rather than rebuilt.
2. Its tail is sized from the DEEPEST reachable tier
   (max_speculative_num_draft_tokens under adaptive) rather than the launch width.
   A tail wider than the active window costs nothing and is already handled:
   lookup writes -1 into every tail slot past the current position and downstream
   drops those. A fixed buffer width is also what keeps the per-state CUDA graphs
   capturing one selection shape.

Shallow tiers (at most 1 draft decode step) still do not REUSE the selection -- there
is nothing to amortise -- but they now CAPTURE on draft-extend. Without that, a swap
from a shallow tier back to a deep one read a buffer holding an older position's
top-k and captured_len: a silent acceptance loss, not an error.

The d76fed293a guard is kept and now compares against the deepest reachable width,
so a config that widens the window without widening the chain still raises at startup.

Off by default: the flag lives on the checkpoint's text_config (absent from the
flash-next checkpoint) or --json-model-override-args, so this changes nothing unless
index sharing is deliberately turned on.
